\begin{document}

\title{Do Patient Metadata Still Help Strong Image Models?\\Representation-Dependent Attenuation of Auxiliary Information in Skin Lesion Classification}

\author{\IEEEauthorblockN{Authors to be confirmed}
\IEEEauthorblockA{Affiliation to be confirmed}}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Fusing patient metadata with lesion images is widely reported to improve skin cancer classification. We ask whether this benefit is a property of the data or of a weak image model. We define the added value of metadata as the out-of-fold reduction in cross-entropy when metadata are added to an image score, and show that its population counterpart, $I(Y;A\mid S)$, decomposes exactly into information the image cannot carry plus a residual that vanishes as the representation $S$ approaches the image. In a pre-registered analysis on HAM10000, added value falls from $\HAMavHSV$ to $\HAMavConvNeXt\times10^{-3}$ nats from colour histograms to ConvNeXt-T, also after adjustment for image source. In two pre-registered replication waves covering \REPindepN{} further datasets, this attenuation held in all \REPindepHone{} after Holm correction, whereas attenuation between ResNet-18 and ConvNeXt-T held in \REPindepHtwo{}. Exploratory manipulations locate the driver: fine-tuning ResNet-18 on 10\% to 100\% of HAM10000 lowered added value from $\FTfracTen$ to $\FTfracFull\times10^{-3}$ nats, whereas larger frozen backbones of the same family changed neither image performance nor added value. The share of the metadata's information that a strong representation leaves non-redundant ranged from \RHOmin{} to \RHOmax{} across datasets, and a pre-registered prediction that clinical photographs leave more of it than dermoscopy of the same lesions failed. Label-informative missingness inflated one dataset's metadata signal; complete-case analyses reproduce the main pattern. An exact spatial permutation test shows that classifier evidence concentrates on expert-segmented lesions and that missed melanomas are smaller rather than mislocalised.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
skin lesion classification, patient metadata, multimodal fusion, conditional mutual information, representation learning, pre-registration, interpretability, spatial permutation test
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
Clinicians do not diagnose skin lesions from images alone: age, anatomical site, and lesion history all shape the assessment. Following this intuition, many automated systems fuse patient metadata with image features. A systematic review of such systems found that every included study reported a gain from patient data, while noting that it remains unclear how each variable contributes~\cite{hohn2021review}. On the smartphone dataset PAD-UFES-20, adding clinical data improved balanced accuracy by about 7\% across several networks~\cite{pacheco2020impact}.

A reported gain, however, conflates two things: information in the metadata that the image genuinely lacks, and information the image contains but a particular model failed to extract. A patient's age, for example, is partly visible in the skin around a lesion. If an image model is weak, metadata can compensate for what it misses; if it is strong, the same metadata may be redundant. Whether metadata ``help'' may therefore depend on the image model they are added to, a dependence that single-backbone studies cannot reveal.

This paper makes five contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{An interpretation of added value.} Using the chain rule of mutual information, we write the Bayes added value of metadata $A$ to a representation $S=g(X)$ of an image $X$ as $I(Y;A\mid S)=I(Y;A\mid X)+R(S)$, where $I(Y;A\mid X)$ is the information unavailable from the image and the residual $R(S)$ vanishes as $S\to X$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp}). The decomposition is elementary; its value is that it says what a measured gain from metadata does and does not show.
\item \textbf{A measurement protocol.} We estimate added value out of fold along a ladder of frozen image representations, with grouped cross-validation, inner-fold tuning, and grouped bootstrap inference, and we audit metadata for label leakage, including label-informative missingness.
\item \textbf{Pre-registered evidence on seven datasets.} A pre-registered analysis on HAM10000~\cite{tschandl2018ham} and two pre-registered replication waves: ISIC 2020~\cite{rotemberg2021isic}, ISIC 2019 excluding HAM10000~\cite{combalia2019bcn} and PAD-UFES-20~\cite{pacheco2020pad}; then HIBA~\cite{riccilara2023hiba}, MSK 2020~\cite{msk2020collection} and MILK10k~\cite{tschandl2026milk}, whose paired dermoscopic and clinical images of the same lesions allow a test of imaging modality. Two pre-registered mechanism tests failed, and we report why.
\item \textbf{Manipulations of representation strength.} Fine-tuning, training-set size, backbone size within a family, and a 304M-parameter vision transformer separate what attenuates metadata value (a better image model for the task) from what does not (more parameters on frozen features).
\item \textbf{A calibrated interpretability test.} We compare class-activation evidence with expert lesion masks using an exact spatial permutation test computed by FFT, and we document two pitfalls: invalid pixel permutation and a sign convention that manufactures an apparent link between errors and off-lesion attention.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}
\textbf{Metadata fusion in dermatology.} Concatenating one-hot metadata with image features is the dominant approach, and all eleven studies in a systematic review reported a gain~\cite{hohn2021review}. The closest prior evidence is a benchmark of nine backbones on an ISIC subset~\cite{ahammed2025metadata}: averaged over its backbones, metadata raised AUROC by 0.023 for models trained from scratch but by only 0.001 for ImageNet-pretrained, fine-tuned models (our calculation from its Table~5; single runs, no intervals). The authors did not frame this as attenuation, and to our knowledge no study has measured added value along a controlled ladder of image models with grouped inference and pre-specified tests. A reader study on MILK10k found dermoscopy and clinical close-ups diagnostically complementary~\cite{muller2026milk}; whether the modality changes what metadata add to an automated model has not been tested.

\textbf{Incremental value.} In clinical prediction, the added value of a new variable is commonly tested with nested models; comparing nested-model AUCs with DeLong's test is invalid~\cite{demler2012}. In logistic models, omitting a predictor that is independent of a covariate still attenuates that covariate's coefficient (non-collapsibility)~\cite{gail1984}. This mechanism makes directly acting metadata gain value as the image model improves (Section~\ref{sec:theory}).

\textbf{Leakage and shortcuts.} Splitting medical images without grouping by subject inflates accuracy substantially~\cite{yagis2021leakage}. Dermatology datasets contain several images per lesion and per patient, and acquisition source can predict diagnosis.

\textbf{Spatial nulls.} Comparisons between spatial maps must preserve spatial autocorrelation in the null distribution; naive voxel permutation is anti-conservative~\cite{alexanderbloch2018,markello2022neuromaps}. We carry this principle over to the evaluation of saliency maps against anatomy.

\section{Theory}\label{sec:theory}
Let $X$ be an image, $A$ metadata, $Y\in\{0,1\}$ the label, and $S=g(X)$ a representation. With log-loss $\ell(y,p)=-\log p(y)$, the Bayes predictors satisfy $\mathbb{E}\,\ell(Y,P(Y\mid S))=H(Y\mid S)$, so the expected reduction in log-loss from adding $A$ is
\begin{equation}
\Delta(S)=H(Y\mid S)-H(Y\mid S,A)=I(Y;A\mid S).\label{eq:delta}
\end{equation}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:decomp}
For any measurable $g$ and $S=g(X)$,
\begin{equation}
I(Y;A\mid S)=I(Y;A\mid X)+\underbrace{I(Y;X\mid S)-I(Y;X\mid S,A)}_{R(S)} .\label{eq:decomp}
\end{equation}
If $g$ is injective, $R(S)=0$. If moreover $Y\Ind A\mid X$, then $\Delta(S)=0$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Expand $I(Y;A,X\mid S)$ with the chain rule in two orders~\cite{cover2006}: $I(Y;A\mid S)+I(Y;X\mid S,A)=I(Y;X\mid S)+I(Y;A\mid X,S)$. Because $S$ is a function of $X$, conditioning on $(X,S)$ equals conditioning on $X$, so $I(Y;A\mid X,S)=I(Y;A\mid X)$. Rearranging gives~\eqref{eq:decomp}. If $g$ is injective, $X$ is a function of $S$, so both terms of $R(S)$ vanish. The final claim follows because $I(Y;A\mid X)=0$ when $Y\Ind A\mid X$.
\end{proof}

\textbf{Interpretation.} $I(Y;A\mid X)$ is the part of the metadata's information that no image model can recover. $R(S)$ measures how the representation's losses interact with $A$. $R(S)>0$ when $A$ substitutes for image information that $S$ discarded; added value then \emph{attenuates} as $S$ improves. $R(S)<0$ indicates synergy; added value then \emph{grows} as $S$ improves, which is the information-theoretic face of non-collapsibility~\cite{gail1984}. Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} was checked numerically to machine precision on 200 random discrete distributions and coarsenings (code release).

\textbf{Estimation.} We estimate $\Delta$ for each rung $k$ of a capacity ladder by a two-stage out-of-fold procedure. First, an image score $s_k(x)$ is obtained from frozen features $f_k(x)$ by $L_2$-regularised logistic regression, with the penalty chosen by grouped inner cross-validation within each training fold. Second, logistic models on $[s_k]$ and $[s_k, a]$ are fitted on the same outer folds, and
\begin{equation}
\hat\Delta_k=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Big[\ell\big(y_i,\hat p^{(-i)}_{0}\big)-\ell\big(y_i,\hat p^{(-i)}_{1}\big)\Big],\label{eq:est}
\end{equation}
where $(-i)$ denotes a model fitted without the fold containing $i$. Because both models are estimated, $\hat\Delta_k$ targets the reduction achievable by the second-stage model family, not $I(Y;A\mid S_k)$ exactly, and it can be negative in finite samples. Uncertainty is quantified by a bootstrap over grouping units (lesions or patients), and differences between rungs by a \emph{paired} grouped bootstrap of per-image loss differences.

\textbf{Interpretability test.} For a linear head $w$ on global-average-pooled features $f=\frac1{|\Omega|}\sum_{u\in\Omega}F(u)$, the class activation map $c(u)=\sum_j w_j F_j(u)$~\cite{zhou2016cam} decomposes the logit exactly. For a non-negative evidence map $e$ and a binary mask $m$ on a periodic grid $\Omega$, the mass inside the mask after a shift by $\tau$ is
\begin{equation}
M(\tau)=\frac{\sum_{u}e(u)\,m(u-\tau)}{\sum_u e(u)}=\frac{(e\star m)(\tau)}{\|e\|_1},
\end{equation}
a circular cross-correlation. Hence $\mathcal{F}^{-1}[\mathcal{F}(e)\overline{\mathcal{F}(m)}]$ yields $M(\tau)$ for all $|\Omega|$ shifts in $O(|\Omega|\log|\Omega|)$, and the exact p-value is $|\{\tau: M(\tau)\ge M(0)\}|/|\Omega|$. Unlike pixel permutation, the shift null preserves the spatial autocorrelation of $e$.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\textbf{Datasets} (Table~\ref{tab:data}). HAM10000 (dermoscopy; 10{,}015 images of 7{,}470 lesions)~\cite{tschandl2018ham}; ISIC 2020 (dermoscopy; 33{,}126 images of 2{,}056 patients)~\cite{rotemberg2021isic}; ISIC 2019 with all HAM10000 lesions removed~\cite{combalia2019bcn}; PAD-UFES-20 (smartphone images; 2{,}298 images of 1{,}373 patients)~\cite{pacheco2020pad}; the dermoscopic images of HIBA~\cite{riccilara2023hiba}; MSK 2020, consecutive biopsies from one centre~\cite{msk2020collection}; and MILK10k, in which every lesion has one dermoscopic and one clinical close-up image~\cite{tschandl2026milk}. The two MILK10k image sets are analysed separately but are not independent. Among datasets that carry ISIC identifiers, no image appears in two datasets; PAD-UFES-20 has no such identifiers.

\textbf{Leakage audit.} Fields that encode how the diagnosis was established were excluded: HAM10000's \texttt{dx\_type} (alone, AUROC \HAMdxtypeAUC{} for melanoma) and PAD-UFES-20's \texttt{biopsed} (every cancer is biopsied). On HAM10000 the image \emph{source} alone predicts melanoma (AUROC \HAMsourceAUC{}), and frozen deep features identify the source almost perfectly (AUROC up to \HAMsourceRecov{}), so we report source-adjusted analyses. A separate audit of \emph{missingness} found that it, too, can carry the label. In PAD-UFES-20, all 804 images lacking gender, skin type, and history fields are benign and unbiopsied, so missingness is a proxy for the excluded \texttt{biopsed} field, and ``unknown'' growth and change histories are 88\% malignant. Smaller associations exist elsewhere (e.g.\ ISIC 2020: no melanoma among the 65 images of unknown sex). As a further control, we refit the second stage after permuting the metadata between grouping units (\PERMR{} permutations). Because the pre-registered encoding keeps missing-value indicators, we add a complete-case sensitivity analysis (Section~\ref{sec:cc}).

\textbf{Ladder.} HSV colour histograms (48-d); ImageNet-pretrained ResNet-18 and ResNet-50~\cite{he2016resnet}, ConvNeXt-T~\cite{liu2022convnext}, and ViT-B/16~\cite{dosovitskiy2021vit}; frozen, pooled features from $224\times224$ centre crops.

\textbf{Protocol.} 5-fold stratified grouped cross-validation (lesion or patient) with three fold assignments; inner grouped 3-fold CV for the penalty; 2{,}000 grouped bootstrap resamples. The HAM10000 analysis and both replication waves were pre-registered, with hypotheses, the Holm correction~\cite{holm1979} and its family (six tests in wave 1, eight in wave 2) fixed before the respective data were modelled. Wave 2 restricted HIBA to dermoscopic images in a dated addendum written after reading its metadata and before any modelling. Additions, all exploratory and labelled as such: the complete-case analysis (prompted by the missingness audit), the retained fraction $\rho$, the source-adjusted and paired HAM10000 analyses, the interpretability analyses, the representation-strength manipulations, and the recoverability, permutation, and cross-dataset analyses; the last group was described in a dated registry before implementation. Two implementation errors found during the work (an image-name mismatch that dropped ISIC 2019 images, and a missing-value check that missed NaN) were fixed before any affected result was used.

