\subsection{Pre-registered analysis on HAM10000}\label{sec:ham}
\input{tables/ham_ladder}
Fig.~\ref{fig:atten} and Table~\ref{tab:ham} show that metadata reduce out-of-fold log-loss by $\HAMavHSV\times10^{-3}$ nats for colour histograms, with a CI excluding zero in all three fold assignments. For the deep representations the reduction is only $\HAMavResEighteen\times10^{-3}$ to $\HAMavConvNeXt\times10^{-3}$ nats, and the log-loss CIs include zero. Table~\ref{tab:hamrobust} expresses the same effect in familiar units: the AUROC gain falls from \HAMdaucHSV{} \HAMdaucHSVCI{} for colour histograms to \HAMdaucResEighteen{} \HAMdaucResEighteenCI{} for ResNet-18, which remains detectable, and to \HAMdaucConvNeXt{} \HAMdaucConvNeXtCI{} for ConvNeXt-T, which does not. Fitting all frozen features and metadata in one model gives nearly the same log-loss as the two-stage design (Table~\ref{tab:hamrobust}), so compressing the image to a one-dimensional score does not hide an interaction.

Recoverability of age from the image rises along the ladder ($R^2$ from \HAMageRecovHSV{} to \HAMageRecovConvNeXt{}), consistent with Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} for metadata whose information the image carries. A per-variable analysis did not sharpen this: age, sex, and site retained \HAMretainedAge{}, \HAMretainedSex{}, and \HAMretainedSite{} of their colour-histogram added value at the strongest rungs, and even sex proved recoverable from dermoscopy (AUROC \HAMsexRecovConvNeXt{}). Because all three variables became recoverable together, HAM10000 alone cannot isolate the mechanism, which motivates the symptom analysis on PAD-UFES-20 below. Three of four pre-registered predictions held; the predicted non-zero floor was not observed.

\input{tables/ham_robust}

\subsection{Shortcut control and paired tests}\label{sec:shortcut}
\input{tables/ham_paired}
With image source included in both models, added value still falls (from $\HAMadjHSV\times10^{-3}$ to $\HAMadjConvNeXt\times10^{-3}$ nats). Within single sources, melanoma AUROC rises with capacity, so the deep representations do read lesions and do not merely detect the source. The paired attenuation from ResNet-18 to ConvNeXt-T is $\HAMpairedRConv\times10^{-3}$ nats (95\% CI \HAMpairedRConvCI{}$\times10^{-3}$). Across all \PERMn{} image sets, the real metadata outperformed every one of \PERMR{} group-level permutations at ConvNeXt-T on complete cases (empirical $p=\PERMp$, the smallest attainable), so the residual added value is not an artefact of fitting extra parameters.

\subsection{Pre-registered replication}\label{sec:rep}
\input{tables/replication}
Table~\ref{tab:rep} and Fig.~\ref{fig:atten} report both waves. After Holm correction within each registered family, H1 (colour histogram vs.\ ConvNeXt-T) held in \REPwaveOneHone{} of 3 datasets in wave~1 and \REPwaveTwoHone{} of 4 image sets in wave~2, that is, in all \REPindepHone{} independent datasets (MILK10k counted once, and as holding only if both of its image sets held). H2 (ResNet-18 vs.\ ConvNeXt-T) held in \REPwaveOneHtwo{} of 3 and \REPwaveTwoHtwo{} of 4 (MSK 2020: $p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=\REPmskHtwoP$), i.e.\ in \REPindepHtwo{} of \REPindepN{} independent datasets. At ConvNeXt-T the remaining added value ($10^{-3}$ nats, 95\% CI) is: \REPfloors{}.

\textbf{Mechanism test 1 (PAD-UFES-20, wave 1).} We predicted that symptoms, being invisible in photographs, would keep their value while demographics attenuated. From HSV to ConvNeXt-T, the demographic block retained \PADmechD{} of its added value and the symptom block \PADmechS{}, so neither M1 nor M2 held. The premise was false: even \emph{within} a diagnostic class, ConvNeXt-T features predict the symptoms with AUROC \PADsymWithinMin{} to \PADsymWithinMax{} (bleeding and elevation are visible).

\subsection{Pre-registered modality and lesion-size tests}\label{sec:p1}
\textbf{Mechanism test 2 (P1, MILK10k).} If metadata substitute for image detail, clinical close-ups, which show less detail than dermoscopy, should leave more of the metadata's information non-redundant. On the same \PoneN{} lesions the opposite held: $\rho=\PoneRhoClin$ for clinical vs.\ \PoneRhoDerm{} for dermoscopic images at ConvNeXt-T, a paired difference of \PoneDiff{} \PoneCI{}. P1 is therefore rejected. A plausible reason is that its premise again failed in our setting: the two image types gave almost identical image-only AUROC (\MILKaucClin{} vs.\ \MILKaucDerm{}), so the clinical representation was not weaker, consistent with a reader study that found the two modalities complementary rather than ranked~\cite{muller2026milk}.

\textbf{P2 (MSK 2020).} Measured lesion diameter added $\PtwoHSV\times10^{-3}$ nats \PtwoHSVCI{} to the colour-histogram model but only $\PtwoCNX\times10^{-3}$ \PtwoCNXCI{} to ConvNeXt-T, a retained share of \PtwoRetained{} (registered threshold $<0.5$). P2 was registered as exploratory because MSK 2020 is small; the result is consistent with the finding below that deep image scores track lesion size.

