\caption{Retained fraction $\rho$ of the metadata's standalone information left non-redundant by ConvNeXt-T, per image set. Circles: all images with 95\% grouped-bootstrap CI (Table~\ref{tab:retained}); diamonds: complete cases (Table~\ref{tab:cc}).}\label{fig:retained}
\end{figure}
\input{tables/retained}

\subsection{How much information remains?}
Normalising $\hat\Delta$ by the metadata's standalone information $\hat I_A$ gives the retained fraction $\rho$ (Table~\ref{tab:retained}, Fig.~\ref{fig:retained}). It falls steeply from colour histograms to the deep representations everywhere, but its level at ConvNeXt-T varies from \RHOmin{} (\RHOminDS{}) to \RHOmax{} (\RHOmaxDS{}). Dermoscopic datasets lie at both ends of this range, so the floor is a property of the dataset, its label definition and its metadata, not of the imaging modality.

\subsection{Complete-case sensitivity analysis}\label{sec:cc}
\input{tables/complete_case}
Restricting every dataset to images with all metadata recorded (Table~\ref{tab:cc}) removes label-informative missingness. H1 holds (uncorrected) in \CCholdHone{} of \CCn{} image sets and H2 in \CCholdHtwo{} (not in \CCfailHtwo{}). On PAD-UFES-20, the standalone information in the metadata falls from $\PADIAleak\times10^{-3}$ to $\PADIAcc\times10^{-3}$ nats, so much of that dataset's apparent metadata signal reflects how missing values arise; its complete cases (\PADccKept{} images, mostly biopsied) are, however, a different population. Complete-case retained fractions at ConvNeXt-T range from \CCRHOmin{} to \CCRHOmax{}.

\subsection{What makes a representation ``strong''?}\label{sec:ft}
\begin{figure}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/dose.pdf}
\caption{Exploratory dose--response on HAM10000 (ResNet-18): added value of metadata vs.\ the share of training lesions available to the image model. All runs on complete cases. Blue: fine-tuned end to end; line through the mean of runs (two subsamples below 100\%), small dots and bars are individual runs with 95\% CIs, numbers are mean image AUROC. Orange: frozen ImageNet features with a retrained head (mean of two subsamples).}\label{fig:dose}
\end{figure}
\input{tables/finetune}
The ladder orders representations by image-only performance but changes architecture, size, and pretraining at once. Four exploratory manipulations separate these factors; their CIs are not corrected for multiplicity.

\textbf{Fine-tuning.} ResNet-18 and ConvNeXt-T were fine-tuned end to end with the same grouped folds and a fixed schedule (AdamW, learning rate $10^{-4}$, six epochs, no early stopping on test folds). On HAM10000, fine-tuning ResNet-18 raised image AUROC to \FThamAUC{} and lowered added value from $\FThamFrozen$ to $\FTham\times10^{-3}$ nats \FThamCI{}, a paired drop of $\FThamDiff\times10^{-3}$ \FThamDiffCI{} (Table~\ref{tab:ft}). For ConvNeXt-T, whose frozen added value was already small, the drop was not detectable \FTcnxDiffCI{}. On HIBA and PAD-UFES-20 the drop was significant for both backbones, although on PAD-UFES-20 most of the value remained (ConvNeXt-T: $\FTpadCNXfrozen$ to $\FTpadCNXft\times10^{-3}$ nats). On ISIC 2019 and MILK10k (dermoscopic; ResNet-18 only), fine-tuning raised image AUROC (\FTIsicAUCfrz{} to \FTIsicAUCft{}; \FTMilkAUCfrz{} to \FTMilkAUCft{}) but added value fell only slightly ($\FTIsicFrozen$ to $\FTIsicFt$; $\FTMilkFrozen$ to $\FTMilkFt\times10^{-3}$ nats), and neither paired difference excluded zero (\FTIsicDiffCI{}; \FTMilkDiffCI{}).

\textbf{Training-set size.} On HAM10000's complete cases, ResNet-18 was fine-tuned on 10\%, 25\%, 50\%, and 100\% of each training fold's lesions, with two independent subsamples below 100\% (\FTfracNruns{} runs). Mean added value fell from $\FTfracTen$ to $\FTfracTwentyFive$, $\FTfracFifty$, and $\FTfracFull\times10^{-3}$ nats (100\%: 95\% CI \FTfracFullCI{}) as image AUROC rose from \FTfracTenAUC{} to \FTfracFullAUC{} (Fig.~\ref{fig:dose}); across runs, the Spearman correlation between training fraction and added value was \FTfracSpearman{}. Between 50\% and 100\%, image AUROC changed little (\FTfracFiftyAUC{} vs.\ \FTfracFullAUC{}) while added value kept falling, although the intervals of adjacent fractions overlap; image AUROC is thus an incomplete summary of representation strength. Retraining only the head of frozen features on the same fractions moved added value in the same direction but less ($\FZhamTen$ to $\FZhamFull$ on HAM10000; $\FZisicTen$ to $\FZisicFull$ on ISIC 2019).

\textbf{Backbone size.} Within families (ResNet-18/34/50/101; ConvNeXt-T/S/B), frozen and on complete cases of \SIZEdatasets{} datasets, none of \SIZEn{} smallest-vs-largest comparisons of added value was significant, and image AUROC changed by only \SIZEaucChangeMin{} to \SIZEaucChangeMax{}.

\textbf{A larger pretrained model.} A frozen ViT-L/16 (304M parameters, weakly supervised SWAG pretraining~\cite{singh2022swag}) changed image AUROC relative to ConvNeXt-T by \VITLaucMin{} to \VITLaucMax{} across \VITLn{} image sets; added value was slightly lower on \VITLlower{} and not higher anywhere.

Together, these results tie attenuation to how well the image model solves the task, not to the number of parameters applied to frozen features.

\subsection{Interpretability: where is the evidence?}
\input{tables/interp}
\input{tables/interp_backbones}
For each backbone we use the exact class activation map of the out-of-fold head. For ConvNeXt-T, whose pooled features pass through a LayerNorm before the head, the map is exact up to a positive per-image factor: with per-image LayerNorm scale $\sigma$ and affine weights $\gamma$, the logit equals a constant plus $\sigma^{-1}|\Omega|^{-1}\sum_u\sum_j \tilde w_j\big(F_j(u)-\bar F(u)\big)$ with $\tilde w_j=w_j\gamma_j/s_j$ and $\bar F(u)$ the channel mean. The overlap statistics are invariant to that factor, and the reconstructed logits matched the head's logits with correlation $1.000$ for every backbone.

Mapping the \emph{predicted} class's evidence produced an apparent link between errors and off-lesion attention ($p<10^{-49}$; Table~\ref{tab:interp}). This is an artefact: for ``not melanoma'' predictions, the evidence for that class lies in the surrounding skin. With the melanoma-evidence map used for every image (Table~\ref{tab:interpbb}), melanoma evidence is concentrated on the lesion in \INTbbEnrichRange{} of images across the three backbones. Missed melanomas show no less on-lesion evidence; instead they are consistently smaller lesions ($p\le\INTbbAreaPmax$ for every backbone). Pixel permutation declares \INTbbPermRange{} of maps significant, against \INTbbShiftRange{} under the exact shift null, consistent with the inflation predicted by its disregard of spatial autocorrelation~\cite{alexanderbloch2018,markello2022neuromaps}.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}
\textbf{What the results mean.} On every independent dataset, metadata add clearly more to a colour-histogram model than to frozen deep representations, and better task-specific image models (fine-tuned, or trained on more data) leave less for the metadata to add, although on ISIC 2019 and MILK10k the drop after fine-tuning was small and not significant. Attenuation \emph{between} frozen deep representations is smaller and inconsistent (H2: \REPindepHtwo{} of \REPindepN{}), as expected if those representations differ little in task performance. How much value remains at a strong representation depends on the dataset, from almost none (HIBA, HAM10000) to about half (PAD-UFES-20, MSK 2020, ISIC 2019), and not on imaging modality: the pre-registered prediction that clinical images leave more value than dermoscopy of the same lesions failed. The frequently reported benefit of metadata~\cite{hohn2021review,pacheco2020impact} is therefore partly a statement about weak image representations, and the size of the part that is not differs severalfold between datasets.

\textbf{Which variables keep their value?} In an exploratory analysis over \RECrows{} dataset--representation--variable combinations (of \RECrowsAll{}; the rest had no significant standalone value), variables that the image predicts well within a diagnostic class kept a smaller share of their value (Spearman $\RECrho$; partial correlation adjusting for standalone value and image AUROC $\RECpartial$, dataset-clustered 95\% CI \RECpartialCI{}; between \RECrungMin{} and \RECrungMax{} within each representation). This is the pattern Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} predicts, but it is correlational. A transparent rule fitted to these quantities did not predict a variable's added value in held-out datasets better than predicting zero (mean absolute error \RULEridge{} vs.\ \RULEzero{} nats); the simple product of standalone value and a typical retained fraction did best (\RULEbase{}). As a yes/no decision (collect if the predicted value is at least $10^{-3}$ nats), that simple product was right in \RULEbaseAcc{} of cases (precision \RULEbasePrec{}, recall \RULEbaseRec{}; Appendix~\ref{app:explor}). With six independent datasets, a reliable quantitative rule is beyond reach.

\textbf{Under dataset shift.} When the image and fusion models were fitted on one dataset and applied to another (harmonised age, sex, and site; \SHIFTn{} directed pairs), metadata improved log-loss significantly in \SHIFThelp{} pairs and worsened it in \SHIFThurt{}, from $\SHIFTmin$ to $\SHIFTmax$ nats. The largest harms occurred between datasets with very different prevalences and label definitions, so these results describe the joint shift rather than a property of metadata.

\textbf{Practical implication.} Metadata fusion should be evaluated against the strongest available image model, ideally one fine-tuned on the target data, and the retained fraction $\rho$ reported alongside the gain, so that a benefit that merely compensates for a weak backbone is not mistaken for complementary information.

\textbf{Limitations.} (i) Fine-tuning was examined on five datasets (two with ResNet-18 only) with one training run per fold and a fixed schedule; the drop in added value was significant on three. (ii) $\hat\Delta$ estimates the reduction achievable by a logistic second stage, not $I(Y;A\mid S)$ itself. (iii) The frozen ladder is ordered by image-only performance and is not a controlled intervention; Section~\ref{sec:ft} addresses this only in part. (iv) The mechanism in Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} is not isolated: both pre-registered mechanism tests failed because their premises (invisible symptoms; a weaker clinical-image representation) did not hold. (v) Images come from public archives and mirrors; official metadata were used and counts were checked against the published descriptions. MSK 2020 has no lesion identifier and is grouped per image; ISIC 2019 images without one are grouped individually. (vi) Missingness is label-informative in every dataset, severely so in PAD-UFES-20; complete-case analysis removes this leak but analyses a selected subsample, and some exploratory PAD-UFES-20 results (Table~\ref{tab:ft}) use all images. (vii) All analyses are retrospective; nothing here is a clinical claim, and no causal effect of metadata is estimated. (viii) Saliency overlap is evidence of spatial correspondence, not of causal reliance~\cite{adebayo2018sanity}.

\section{Conclusion}
The value of patient metadata to a skin lesion classifier is not fixed: it depends on what the image model already extracts. Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} makes this precise. Pre-registered experiments on seven datasets show that metadata add far less to deep representations than to colour histograms, and exploratory manipulations show that fine-tuning and more training data tend to shrink the gain further, while more parameters on frozen features do not. Attenuation among frozen deep representations did not replicate consistently, the share of metadata information left non-redundant ranged severalfold across datasets, and imaging modality did not explain that range. Claims that metadata improve a classifier should therefore be made against strong, task-adapted image baselines and accompanied by the retained fraction.

