\section{Simulation of the attenuation signature}\label{app:sim}
\input{tables/app_sim}
In a simulation with a known answer (E09), a latent image variable generated the label, and an auxiliary variable acted either through the image, directly on the label, both, or not at all. A ladder of image models was created by adding decreasing noise to the image representation. Added value fell to zero when the auxiliary variable acted through the image, as its recoverability from the image rose, and \emph{rose} when it acted directly (Table~\ref{tab:sim}); the latter is the non-collapsibility of logistic regression~\cite{gail1984} and corresponds to $R(S)<0$ in Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp}. The mixed case fell to a non-zero floor.

\section{Further manipulations of the image representation}\label{app:strength}
\input{tables/app_family}
\input{tables/app_scaling}
\input{tables/app_vitl}
\input{tables/app_res}
Tables~\ref{tab:family}--\ref{tab:vitl} give the full results summarised in Section~\ref{sec:ft}. Table~\ref{tab:res} reports a resolution ladder (E19): with the architecture fixed, images were downsampled before embedding. The prediction that added value falls monotonically with resolution held in \RESholdsN{} of the \RESn{} image sets analysed (\RESholds{}; the PAD-UFES-20 analysis uses all images and therefore includes its missingness leak), and image AUROC hardly changed (on HAM10000 it is 0.88 even at $16\times16$ pixels), so resolution is a weak handle on representation strength for these tasks.

\section{Per-variable floors}\label{app:pervar}
\input{tables/app_pervar}
Single-variable added values (Table~\ref{tab:pervar}) show that every variable with detectable value at the colour-histogram rung attenuates on HAM10000 and ISIC 2020 (on ISIC 2020, sex and site carry no detectable value at all), whereas on ISIC 2019 anatomical site keeps most of its value. This is consistent with ISIC 2019's large non-redundant share (Table~\ref{tab:retained}) and with BCN20000's deliberate inclusion of hard-to-diagnose sites~\cite{combalia2019bcn}.

\section{Exploratory analyses}\label{app:explor}
\begin{figure*}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/recoverability.pdf}
\caption{Exploratory (E30): retained share of each variable's added value ($\hat\Delta_{\mathrm{var}}/\hat I_{\mathrm{var}}$) against its within-class recoverability from the image representation, for the \RECrows{} combinations where the variable's standalone value was significant. Orange: colour histogram; blue: deep representations.}\label{fig:recov}
\end{figure*}
\input{tables/app_perm}
\input{tables/app_rule}
\input{tables/app_shift}
\textbf{Recoverability} (Fig.~\ref{fig:recov}). The negative association reported in Section~VI does not depend on the colour histogram: restricted to the deep representations, the within-dataset Spearman correlation is negative in \RECdeepNeg{} of \RECdeepN{} image sets (from \RECdeepMin{} to \RECdeepMax{}; as few as \RECdeepMinRows{} points in some sets). The rule excluding rows with non-significant standalone value was fixed in the analysis code before the run but was not stated in the dated registry.

\textbf{Permutation control} (Table~\ref{tab:perm}). Permuting whole metadata records between grouping units destroys their link to the image and label but keeps their joint distribution; permuted metadata never matched the real metadata's added value and always had negative added value on average, as expected from fitting extra parameters to noise.

\textbf{Collection rule} (Table~\ref{tab:rule}). A ridge model on six cheap indicators was worse than predicting zero; the product of standalone value and the median retained share of the training datasets was the best predictor and made a useful collect/skip decision.

\textbf{Dataset shift} (Table~\ref{tab:shift}). Under shift, metadata fitted on the source frequently harmed the target, most strongly between datasets with very different prevalences. Within HAM10000, holding out one image source at a time ($10^{-3}$ nats, 95\% CI): \LOSOtext{}.

